# Copy to `.env` (NOT `.env.local` — the Prisma CLI does not read `.env.local`,
# so splitting them yields a working app and a failing migration).
#
# ONE SHARED SUPABASE PROJECT, ONE SCHEMA PER APP. Cadence owns `cadence` and
# touches nothing in `paste`, `hacker_news_clone` or `public`. Take both URLs
# from another app in this workspace and change only the schema name.
#
# THE TWO URLS DIFFER BY ONE QUERY PARAMETER, AND IT IS NOT COSMETIC:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  No `?schema=`. With it, Prisma treats cadence as the
#                 connection's default and emits UNQUALIFIED SQL, which then
#                 resolves against the pg driver's real search_path
#                 (`public, extensions` — `?schema=` is a Prisma concept libpq
#                 ignores) and fails with `relation "feeds" does not exist`.
#                 Without it, the generated client qualifies every table.
#
#   DIRECT_URL    WITH `?schema=cadence`. The migrate CLI reads this to know
#                 which schema to target; omit it and `_prisma_migrations`
#                 lands in `public` of the shared project.
#
# THEY ALSO DIFFER BY PORT IN DEPLOYMENT, and the pair below does not show it
# because the transaction pooler is unreachable from here:
#
#   :6543  TRANSACTION pooler — DATABASE_URL in deployment. Each serverless
#          invocation is its own process with its own pool, so a session-mode
#          connection per invocation exhausts the pooler. `?pgbouncer=true`
#          goes with it. (That parameter is a Prisma concept the pg driver
#          ignores, exactly like `?schema=` above — harmless, and kept for the
#          next person who greps for it rather than as something that acts.)
#
#   :5432  SESSION pooler — DIRECT_URL always, because `prisma migrate` cannot
#          run over transaction-mode pooling. And DATABASE_URL too when 6543 is
#          blocked, which it is on at least one network here: measured
#          `ETIMEDOUT` to all three pooler addresses on 6543 while 5432
#          answered. `paste/.env.example` documents the same fallback.
#
# THE LOCAL FALLBACK HAS A COST WORTH KNOWING. The session pooler allows 15
# clients for the WHOLE SHARED PROJECT — paste and hacker_news_clone draw on
# the same 15 — and each test file is a process holding up to DATABASE_POOL_MAX.
# That is why `vitest.config.mts` caps workers at 4, and why raising it is a
# decision about the other apps too, not just this one. On 6543 the ceiling is
# far higher and the cap could go.
#
# A wrong password reads as `28P01` and a blocked port as `ETIMEDOUT`. Do not
# rotate credentials at a firewall.

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres?schema=cadence"

# Connections this process may hold. Optional; defaults to 3. The budget is per
# PROCESS and Next runs several render workers, so the real ceiling is
# `workers x max`. An empty or unparseable value falls back to 3 rather than 0.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=3

# Lets `pnpm test` CREATE and DELETE rows, which the constraint probes need.
# Opt-in on purpose: the project is shared, so `pnpm check` stays read-only.
# `pnpm test:db` sets this for you.
# CADENCE_DB_WRITE_TESTS=1

# The shared secret the pinger authenticates with (ARCHITECTURE §6.4).
#
# /api/cron/poll is a WRITE endpoint reachable by anyone who knows the path.
# Unauthenticated it is a free amplifier: a stranger makes this server fetch
# every subscribed feed as often as they can call it, and the publishers see
# OUR User-Agent, not theirs.
#
# THE ROUTE FAILS CLOSED. With this unset it answers 503 to every request,
# including in development. There is no NODE_ENV escape hatch — an endpoint
# that is open "until the secret is configured" is open on the first deploy,
# which is exactly when nobody is looking.
#
# Generate one, and set the SAME value in two places:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   - this project's Vercel environment variables
#   - the GitHub repository secret CRON_SECRET (see .github/workflows/ping.yml,
#     which also needs CRON_ENDPOINT)
CRON_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clerk — accounts (M11)
#
# BOTH ARE REQUIRED EVERYWHERE, including in development, and unlike
# CRON_SECRET there is no failing closed: `clerkMiddleware()` in src/proxy.ts
# THROWS when either key is missing, and its matcher covers every route. So a
# deployment without this pair answers 500 to everything — the reader, the
# health screen, and /api/cron/poll, which means the sweep stops too and the
# only symptom is feeds quietly going stale.
#
# Written by `clerk init`, which links the project to a Clerk application and
# pulls that application's DEVELOPMENT keys. To restore them on a fresh clone:
#
#   pnpm dlx clerk@latest auth login     # once per machine, not per project
#   pnpm dlx clerk@latest env pull       # development keys for the linked app
#
# `env pull` without `--print` OVERWRITES these in place, which only matters if
# a production instance ever exists; see below.
#
# THESE DEVELOPMENT KEYS ARE THE ONLY PAIR, deliberately. Cadence stays on
# Clerk's development instance — there is no production one and no `clerk
# deploy` step at M15 (see the memo's decisions.md). The cost is a "Development
# mode" badge on the sign-in card and strict usage caps, which is the ceiling on
# how many people can use this: fine for one reader, not for a shared one.
# The same pair goes in the Vercel environment variables when it deploys.
#
# The publishable key is NEXT_PUBLIC_ and ships to the browser by design; it
# identifies the Clerk instance and encodes its frontend origin. The secret key
# never reaches client code.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=""

# Where Clerk sends a visitor who needs to sign in, and where to land them
# afterwards. Set explicitly rather than left to Clerk's hosted pages, because
# the sign-in screen is part of this app's design system.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL="/sign-in"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL="/sign-up"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL="/reader"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL="/reader"

# OPTIONAL, development only, and never committed: your own Clerk user id.
#
# Every subscription, folder and read mark in the fixture corpus belongs to the
# fixture reader, so a real account signing in owns nothing — empty sidebar,
# zero unread, a product that looks broken through a scoping rule working
# exactly as designed. Set this and `pnpm db:seed` clones the fixture reader's
# rows for your id as well, sharing the same `feeds` and `articles`.
#
# Find it with `pnpm dlx clerk@latest users list --json`, or in the Clerk
# dashboard. Leave it unset and the seed skips the whole step, which is what
# CI and a fresh clone want.
#
# NO VISIBLE EFFECT YET: `getViewer()` still returns the fixture reader, so
# signing in shows that reader's screen whoever you are. These rows exist so
# the step that resolves the Clerk session has something to resolve to.
CADENCE_DEV_USER_ID=""
